## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of CQRS pattern in real-time collaborative apps - crucial for Miro's architecture where immediate UI feedback and data consistency are both required.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidate will explain: Command side receives shape update request, validates permissions, persists to database, emits ShapeUpdated event. Query side maintains read model for fast UI rendering. Event-driven architecture ensures eventual consistency across all connected clients.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Command
class UpdateShapePositionCommand {
  constructor(public shapeId: string, public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

// Command Handler
class ShapeCommandHandler {
  async handle(command: UpdateShapePositionCommand) {
    const shape = await this.repo.findById(command.shapeId);
    shape.updatePosition(command.x, command.y);
    await this.repo.save(shape);

    // Emit event for query side
    this.eventBus.emit(new ShapeUpdated(shape.id, shape.x, shape.y));
  }
}

// Query side optimized for UI
class ShapeReadModel {
  async getShape(shapeId: string) {
    return this.readDb.shapes.find(shapeId);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle concurrent edits from multiple users?
- What happens if the command fails but the event was already emitted?
- How would you scale the read side for millions of users?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design distributed workflows across microservices with CQRS/ES, ensuring tenant isolation, idempotency, and reliable message delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Long-running sagas with compensating transactions for rollback scenarios
- CQRS and Event Sourcing with versioned events for auditability and replay
- Outbox pattern ensuring at-least-once delivery
- Idempotency via correlationId and deduplication checks
- Per-tenant read models maintaining data isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Event-to-command mapping with deduplication
function handleEvent(evt, state) {
  if (state.processed?.includes(evt.id)) return;

  const cmd = mapEventToCommand(evt);
  enqueueToOutbox(cmd);
  state.processed = [...(state.processed || []), evt.id];
}

// Saga orchestration with compensation
async function executeOnboardSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  try {
    await TenantAuth.create(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Billing.setupQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
    await DataIngestion.provision(tenantId, correlationId);
    await Analytics.initialize(tenantId, correlationId);
  } catch (error) {
    await compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId);
  }
}

async function compensateSaga(tenantId, correlationId) {
  await Analytics.deprovision(tenantId, correlationId);
  await DataIngestion.stop(tenantId, correlationId);
  await Billing.revertQuota(tenantId, correlationId);
  await TenantAuth.delete(tenantId, correlationId);
}
```
